  "configuration_id": "config_009",
  "reason": "With no prior experiments, the key uncertainty is whether a multimodal setup provides a meaningful accuracy baseline and at what cost. Config_009 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, weighted fusion) is the simplest multimodal configuration in the space: it uses the cheapest RNA encoder (PCA, no learned encoder), a standard mid-size image encoder (ResNet50 rather than the more expensive ViT-L), single-profile (no multiprofile aggregation overhead), and a straightforward weighted fusion. This establishes a clear multimodal accuracy baseline at moderate expected cost (roughly 1x relative to the cheapest single-modality runs, and well below multiprofile+ViL configurations). It is the best first experiment because it directly tests the core hypothesis of the study (does combining RNA and image features help?) at the lowest multimodal cost, giving a reference point against which I can later evaluate whether multiprofile aggregation, richer encoders (scgpt/scvi), transformer aggregation, or ViT-L images are worth their additional GPU hours."
}

